{"toolCallId":"call_cc32e138c18944d79c4c9fdff5f926ac","title":"Searched for MIN_\\|minimum\\|threshold\\|words in ./.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.js","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"grep -n \"MIN_\\|minimum\\|threshold\\|words\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.js","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"grep -n \"MIN_\\|minimum\\|threshold\\|words\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.js | head -40","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["grep"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_d0b86dfbada34cc0b784531682f07ef8","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh always prints a full report, so a caller that needs only the level has to grep or awk it out of the output. A --quiet flag that prints just the recommended-level line lets the script compose in shell pipelines, while the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"recommend-level quiet mode\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` is the scoring entry point for documentation level, and every run prints a full report: a leading blank line, the level line, score and confidence, a six-line breakdown, and an optional phase section. A caller that needs only the level — a shell prompt, a CI step, an intake wrapper — has to parse that report. This packet adds a `--quiet` flag that prints the existing `Recommended Level: N (Name)` line and nothing else, leaving the default report byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` reuses the human-readable level line the script already prints rather than inventing a bare-value format; `--json` keeps precedence when both flags are present.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: the existing `output_text()` function and argument parser in `recommend-level.sh`, and Bash 3.2 compatibility (the macOS default `/bin/bash`).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Planned |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` has no way to emit a single value. Run with `--loc 50 --files 2` it prints 16 lines, of which exactly one — `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)` — answers the caller's question. Passing `--quiet` today fails with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet` and exit 1, so a caller either parses the report with `grep` or `awk`, or switches to `--json` and parses a document. Both approaches couple the caller to output details the flag should hide.\n\n### Purpose\n\nLet a caller read the recommended level with one line of shell and no parsing, without changing any existing output.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- `--quiet` in `recommend-level.sh`: the parser branch, a `QUIET_MODE` global, and an early return in `output_text()`.\n- The script's own documentation of the flag: the header OPTIONS block and `show_help()`.\n- These planning documents.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- `-q` short alias — the request names `--quiet` only; sibling scripts use `--quiet|-q`, recorded as open question Q1.\n- `SPECKIT_QUIET` environment override — `validate.sh` has one, but `recommend-level.sh` exposes no environment surface at all today.\n- JSON shape changes — `--json` output stays byte-identical.\n- External reference docs (`feature-catalog/`, `references/`) — they describe the scoring lifecycle rather than a per-flag inventory, and the in-script help is the flag's reference.\n- New test files — the behavior is pinned by the command checks in `tasks.md` and by the existing legacy suites that already execute this script.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add `--quiet` parsing, a `QUIET_MODE` global, a single-line early return in `output_text()`, and the header/help entries |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--quiet` prints exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, with no leading or trailing blank line. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the current output for every existing flag combination. |\n| REQ-003 | `--quiet` composes with existing flags in any order, including `--no-recommend-phases`, `--phase-threshold`, and the risk flags. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | The flag is listed in the header OPTIONS block and in `show_help()`; `--help` still prints the full help text when `--quiet` is also passed. |\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` suppresses success output only: every existing error still goes to stderr with its current message and exit code 1. |\n| REQ-006 | With `--json`, the JSON document is emitted unchanged and `--quiet` has no effect. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2 --quiet` emits one line matching `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)`; `wc -l` reports 1.\n- **SC-002**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2` emits the same 16 lines as the pre-change baseline, with an empty `diff`.\n- **SC-003**: `--help` lists `--quiet`, and `--quiet --help` prints the same full help.\n- **SC-004**: the existing phase suites still pass: `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` and `test-phase-system.js`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `output_text()` (`recommend-level.sh:508-558`) | The guard has no clean insertion point if the function is restructured first | Place the guard as the first statement and keep the function's single-exit shape |\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 | Newer syntax would fail on macOS `/bin/bash` | Use only constructs the file already contains |\n| Risk | Quiet output leaks a blank line or the score line | Med | The guard returns before the first `echo \"\"`; AC-001 asserts one line |\n| Risk | Default output drifts while the flag is added | Med | AC-002 and AC-003 diff the captured baseline against post-change output |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: One extra string comparison per run; no measurable change to a script that finishes in well under 100 ms.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: No new input surface beyond a boolean flag; no secret, credential, or network access is introduced.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: Exit codes unchanged — 0 on success, 1 on usage error.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` keeps the existing `At least --loc or --files must be provided` error and exit 1.\n- Repeated flag: `--quiet --quiet` is idempotent.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- Invalid numeric input (`--quiet --loc abc`): the existing error goes to stderr with exit 1; quiet does not swallow it.\n- Unknown option alongside `--quiet`: the existing `ERROR: Unknown option` message, exit 1.\n- Flag ordering: `--quiet` before, between, or after other flags produces the same single line.\n- JSON combination: `--json --quiet` emits the unchanged JSON document.\n- Help combination: `--help --quiet` prints the full help and exits 0.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 5/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~8, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 2/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N |\n| Research | 2/20 | Read one script; no external investigation |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 1/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **10/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe scoring model puts this change at Level 0: `--loc 10 --files 1` scores 2/100, because one shell file with no risk flags is the cheapest shape the model knows. The packet is filed at Level 3 because the packet author chose that depth for authoring-benchmark coverage, not because the change is complex. The documents stay short where the work is small.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The guard is placed after the leading blank line, so quiet output starts with a newline | M | M | Guard is the first statement in `output_text()`; AC-001 asserts one line |\n| R-002 | A later refactor of `output_text()` drops the guard silently | M | L | AC-010 runs the suites; the flag is documented in help |\n| R-003 | `--json --quiet` surprises a caller expecting the bare line | L | M | Help states the JSON precedence; ADR-001 records it |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Read the level in a pipeline (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** shell user composing a spec workflow, **I want** `--quiet` to print only the level line, **so that** I can capture it with `$(...)` without `grep` or `awk`.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows AC-001 through AC-005).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Discover the flag from help (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** maintainer reading `--help`, **I want** the flag listed with the others, **so that** I do not have to read the script to learn it exists.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-006).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Q1: Should `--quiet` also accept `-q`, matching `validate.sh`, `check-completion.sh` and `progressive-validate.sh`? Deferred: the request names `--quiet` only, and an alias is a separate behavior change.\n- Q2: Should `recommend-level.sh` gain a `SPECKIT_QUIET` environment override like `validate.sh`? Deferred: the script has no environment surface today.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See the ADR section of `plan.md` (this packet keeps decisions inline; the Level 3 file contract does not require `decision-record.md`)\n\n---\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh always prints a full report, so a caller that needs only the level has to grep or awk it out of the output. A --quiet flag that prints just the recommended-level line lets the script compose in shell pipelines, while the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"recommend-level quiet mode\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` is the scoring entry point for documentation level, and every run prints a full report: a leading blank line, the level line, score and confidence, a six-line breakdown, and an optional phase section. A caller that needs only the level — a shell prompt, a CI step, an intake wrapper — has to parse that report. This packet adds a `--quiet` flag that prints the existing `Recommended Level: N (Name)` line and nothing else, leaving the default report byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` reuses the human-readable level line the script already prints rather than inventing a bare-value format; `--json` keeps precedence when both flags are present.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: the existing `output_text()` function and argument parser in `recommend-level.sh`, and Bash 3.2 compatibility (the macOS default `/bin/bash`).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Planned |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` has no way to emit a single value. Run with `--loc 50 --files 2` it prints 16 lines, of which exactly one — `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)` — answers the caller's question. Passing `--quiet` today fails with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet` and exit 1, so a caller either parses the report with `grep` or `awk`, or switches to `--json` and parses a document. Both approaches couple the caller to output details the flag should hide.\n\n### Purpose\n\nLet a caller read the recommended level with one line of shell and no parsing, without changing any existing output.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- `--quiet` in `recommend-level.sh`: the parser branch, a `QUIET_MODE` global, and an early return in `output_text()`.\n- The script's own documentation of the flag: the header OPTIONS block and `show_help()`.\n- These planning documents.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- `-q` short alias — the request names `--quiet` only; sibling scripts use `--quiet|-q`, recorded as open question Q1.\n- `SPECKIT_QUIET` environment override — `validate.sh` has one, but `recommend-level.sh` exposes no environment surface at all today.\n- JSON shape changes — `--json` output stays byte-identical.\n- External reference docs (`feature-catalog/`, `references/`) — they describe the scoring lifecycle rather than a per-flag inventory, and the in-script help is the flag's reference.\n- New test files — the behavior is pinned by the command checks in `tasks.md` and by the existing legacy suites that already execute this script.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add `--quiet` parsing, a `QUIET_MODE` global, a single-line early return in `output_text()`, and the header/help entries |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--quiet` prints exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, with no leading or trailing blank line. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the current output for every existing flag combination. |\n| REQ-003 | `--quiet` composes with existing flags in any order, including `--no-recommend-phases`, `--phase-threshold`, and the risk flags. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | The flag is listed in the header OPTIONS block and in `show_help()`; `--help` still prints the full help text when `--quiet` is also passed. |\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` suppresses success output only: every existing error still goes to stderr with its current message and exit code 1. |\n| REQ-006 | With `--json`, the JSON document is emitted unchanged and `--quiet` has no effect. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2 --quiet` emits one line matching `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)`; `wc -l` reports 1.\n- **SC-002**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2` emits the same 16 lines as the pre-change baseline, with an empty `diff`.\n- **SC-003**: `--help` lists `--quiet`, and `--quiet --help` prints the same full help.\n- **SC-004**: the existing phase suites still pass: `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` and `test-phase-system.js`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `output_text()` (`recommend-level.sh:508-558`) | The guard has no clean insertion point if the function is restructured first | Place the guard as the first statement and keep the function's single-exit shape |\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 | Newer syntax would fail on macOS `/bin/bash` | Use only constructs the file already contains |\n| Risk | Quiet output leaks a blank line or the score line | Med | The guard returns before the first `echo \"\"`; AC-001 asserts one line |\n| Risk | Default output drifts while the flag is added | Med | AC-002 and AC-003 diff the captured baseline against post-change output |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: One extra string comparison per run; no measurable change to a script that finishes in well under 100 ms.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: No new input surface beyond a boolean flag; no secret, credential, or network access is introduced.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: Exit codes unchanged — 0 on success, 1 on usage error.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` keeps the existing `At least --loc or --files must be provided` error and exit 1.\n- Repeated flag: `--quiet --quiet` is idempotent.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- Invalid numeric input (`--quiet --loc abc`): the existing error goes to stderr with exit 1; quiet does not swallow it.\n- Unknown option alongside `--quiet`: the existing `ERROR: Unknown option` message, exit 1.\n- Flag ordering: `--quiet` before, between, or after other flags produces the same single line.\n- JSON combination: `--json --quiet` emits the unchanged JSON document.\n- Help combination: `--help --quiet` prints the full help and exits 0.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 5/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~12, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 2/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N |\n| Research | 2/20 | Read one script; no external investigation |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 1/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **10/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe scoring model puts this change at Level 0: `--loc 10 --files 1` scores 2/100, because one shell file with no risk flags is the cheapest shape the model knows. The packet is filed at Level 3 because the packet author chose that depth for authoring-benchmark coverage, not because the change is complex. The documents stay short where the work is small.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The guard is placed after the leading blank line, so quiet output starts with a newline | M | M | Guard is the first statement in `output_text()`; AC-001 asserts one line |\n| R-002 | A later refactor of `output_text()` drops the guard silently | M | L | AC-010 runs the suites; the flag is documented in help |\n| R-003 | `--json --quiet` surprises a caller expecting the bare line | L | M | Help states the JSON precedence; ADR-001 records it |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Read the level in a pipeline (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** shell user composing a spec workflow, **I want** `--quiet` to print only the level line, **so that** I can capture it with `$(...)` without `grep` or `awk`.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows AC-001 through AC-005).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Discover the flag from help (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** maintainer reading `--help`, **I want** the flag listed with the others, **so that** I do not have to read the script to learn it exists.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-006).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Q1: Should `--quiet` also accept `-q`, matching `validate.sh`, `check-completion.sh` and `progressive-validate.sh`? Deferred: the request names `--quiet` only, and an alias is a separate behavior change.\n- Q2: Should `recommend-level.sh` gain a `SPECKIT_QUIET` environment override like `validate.sh`? Deferred: the script has no environment surface today.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See the ADR section of `plan.md` (this packet keeps decisions inline; the Level 3 file contract does not require `decision-record.md`)\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_d3ae72ae49304c2687edd542d345953d","title":"Ran grep","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"grep -rn \"QUIET\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/*.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/*.sh 2>/dev/null","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"grep -rn \"QUIET\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/*.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/*.sh 2>/dev/null | head -20","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["grep"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_e0ddf27689804a5292132b9129e067a9","title":"Ran bash, grep","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-placeholders.sh specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag --verbose 2>&1 | grep -E \"plan.md|spec.md\"","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-placeholders.sh specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag --verbose 2>&1 | grep -E \"plan.md|spec.md\"","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["bash","grep"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_e4e6396408e84fa39872c8f3f06f62da","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Task Format: T### [P?] Description (file path)\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag tasks\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.2 -->\n# Tasks: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n## Task Notation\n\n| Prefix | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `[ ]` | Pending |\n| `[x]` | Completed |\n| `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n| `[B]` | Blocked |\n\n**Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path)`\n\nEvery implementation task touches the same file, `recommend-level.sh`, so `[P]` marks only work that is independent of the code edit — baseline captures, environment checks, and the read-back review.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n## Phase 1: Setup\n\n- [ ] T001 Read `recommend-level.sh` end to end and map the output path: parser `:656-719`, `output_text()` `:508-558`, `output_json()` `:562-649`, dispatch `:770-774` (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`)\n- [ ] T002 Capture the pre-change baselines in `scratch/`: default text for `--loc 50 --files 2`, `--no-recommend-phases` text, and `--json` (scratch/baseline-text.txt, baseline-no-phases.txt, baseline-json.txt)\n- [ ] T003 [P] Record today's rejection of the flag: `--quiet --loc 50 --files 2` prints `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet` and exits 1 (scratch/baseline-quiet-rejected.txt)\n- [ ] T004 [P] Confirm the target shell and syntax budget: `bash --version` on macOS reports 3.2; the change adds no construct the file does not already use (recommend-level.sh)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n## Phase 2: Implementation\n\n- [ ] T005 Add `QUIET_MODE=false` beside `JSON_OUTPUT=false` in the globals block (recommend-level.sh:116-142)\n- [ ] T006 Add the `--quiet)` case to the argument parser, setting `QUIET_MODE=true` and shifting once (recommend-level.sh:656-719)\n- [ ] T007 Add the guard as the first statement in `output_text()`: echo `Recommended Level: ${RECOMMENDED_LEVEL} (${LEVEL_NAME})` and `return 0` before the leading blank line (recommend-level.sh:508-558)\n- [ ] T008 Add the `--quiet` row to the header OPTIONS block, aligned with the existing rows (recommend-level.sh:16-27)\n- [ ] T009 Add the `--quiet` row and one example to `show_help()`, including the sentence that `--json` takes precedence (recommend-level.sh:162-211)\n- [ ] T010 [P] Re-read the finished diff and confirm no scoring, JSON, phase, or exit-code line changed (recommend-level.sh)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n## Phase 3: Verification\n\n- [ ] T011 Run the matrix — flag position (leading, trailing) × text default, `--no-recommend-phases`, `--json` — and assert one line for every quiet text run (scratch/matrix.md)\n- [ ] T012 Diff default text, `--no-recommend-phases` text and `--json` against the T002 baselines; every diff must be empty (scratch/)\n- [ ] T013 Run the error paths with `--quiet` present: `--loc abc`, no inputs, and an unknown option; each must exit 1 with its existing stderr message (scratch/)\n- [ ] T014 Confirm `--help` lists `--quiet` and that `--quiet --help` is byte-identical to `--help` (scratch/)\n- [ ] T015 Run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` and `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js`; both must exit 0 (tests/)\n- [ ] T016 Record evidence in the Verification cell of each criterion and flip AC-001 to AC-010 to `Met` as each is proven (acceptance-criteria.md)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n\n- [ ] T001 is done before any edit: the parser, `output_text()` and the dispatch site are read in the current file, not from memory\n- [ ] T002 baselines are captured before T005 to T009, because a baseline taken after the edit proves nothing\n- [ ] The edit stays inside `recommend-level.sh`; no scoring constant, JSON field, or phase rule is touched\n- [ ] Bash 3.2 is the syntax ceiling: no `mapfile`, no associative arrays, no `${var,,}`\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Requirement |\n|------|-------------|\n| TASK-SEQ | T002 gates T005 onward; T005 to T009 land in one change so the diff reads as one behavior |\n| TASK-SCOPE | Only `recommend-level.sh`; the header and help text are in that file, so no doc file is edited |\n| TASK-EVIDENCE | Each completed task records the command and its observed output in `scratch/` and then in the acceptance row |\n| TASK-BYTE | Default output is compared byte for byte, never by eye or by line count alone |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\n\nReport per task as `T### status (pending/active/done/blocked) — evidence pointer — blocker if any`. A verification task also names the command that produced the evidence.\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\n\nOn a blocked task: mark it `[B]`, record the blocker in this file and in the acceptance criteria, halt dependent tasks, and escalate with the conflicting facts and the decision needed. Never substitute a hand-parsed report for the `--quiet` output the criterion names.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n## Completion Criteria\n\n- [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n- [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n- [ ] SC-001 to SC-004 evidenced in `acceptance-criteria.md`\n<!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n## Cross-References\n\n- **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n- **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Decisions**: See the ADR section of `plan.md`\n<!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n\n---\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n## Verification Protocol\n\n| Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n|----------|----------|-------------------|\n| **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n| **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n| **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n## Pre-Implementation\n\n- [x] CHK-001 [P0] Requirements documented in spec.md\n- [x] CHK-002 [P0] Technical approach defined in plan.md\n- [x] CHK-003 [P1] Dependencies identified and available — Bash 3.2 is the target shell and no external package is added\n<!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n## Code Quality\n\n- [ ] CHK-010 [P0] Code passes lint/format checks — `bash -n recommend-level.sh`, plus `shellcheck` when it is installed\n- [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No console errors or warnings — a quiet run prints one line and nothing on stderr\n- [ ] CHK-012 [P1] Error handling implemented — the guard sits in `output_text()` only, so `exit 1` paths keep their messages\n- [ ] CHK-013 [P1] Code follows project patterns — the flag is a global set in the parser and read at render time, the shape `JSON_OUTPUT` already uses\n<!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## Testing Checklist\n\n- [ ] CHK-020 [P0] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] CHK-021 [P0] Manual testing complete — T011 matrix executed\n- [ ] CHK-022 [P1] Edge cases tested — repeated flag, leading and trailing position, `--help` combination\n- [ ] CHK-023 [P1] Error scenarios validated — T013 exit codes and stderr messages\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n## Fix Completeness\n\n- [x] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Finding class recorded — N/A: this is a feature addition, not a review finding\n- [x] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producer inventory completed — `rg -n 'JSON_OUTPUT|QUIET' recommend-level.sh` finds one text producer and one JSON producer, both listed in `plan.md` affected surfaces\n- [x] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Consumer inventory completed — `rg -n 'recommend-level\\.sh' .skilled --glob '*.js' --glob '*.sh'` finds the two legacy suites; neither passes `--quiet`\n- [x] CHK-FIX-004 [P0] Adversarial table tests — N/A: no path, redaction, or parser-security surface; `--quiet` takes no value\n- [x] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] Matrix axes and row count listed — `plan.md` affected surfaces names the four axes; T011 enumerates the rows\n- [x] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] Hostile env/global-state variant — N/A: the script reads no environment variable and `QUIET_MODE` is script-local\n- [ ] CHK-FIX-007 [P1] Evidence pinned to the change commit rather than a moving branch range\n<!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n## Security\n\n- [x] CHK-030 [P0] No hardcoded secrets — the change adds a boolean and two help lines\n- [ ] CHK-031 [P0] Input validation implemented — the flag takes no value; the existing numeric validation stays unchanged and AC-007 proves it still fires\n- [x] CHK-032 [P1] Auth/authz working correctly — N/A: no auth surface in this script\n<!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n## Documentation\n\n- [x] CHK-040 [P1] Spec/plan/tasks synchronized\n- [ ] CHK-041 [P1] Code comments adequate — T008 and T009 add the flag to the header block and `show_help()`\n- [x] CHK-042 [P2] README updated — N/A: the in-script help is the flag's reference; no README lists per-flag options\n<!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n## File Organization\n\n- [x] CHK-050 [P1] Temp files in scratch/ only — baselines and the matrix record live in `scratch/`\n- [ ] CHK-051 [P1] scratch/ cleaned before completion\n<!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## Verification Summary\n\n| Category | Total | Verified |\n|----------|-------|----------|\n| P0 Items | 15 | 0/15 |\n| P1 Items | 23 | 0/23 |\n| P2 Items | 9 | 0/9 |\n\n**Verification Date**: 2026-10-26\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n## L3+: Architecture Verification\n\n- [x] CHK-100 [P0] Architecture decisions documented — ADR-001 and ADR-002 in `plan.md`; this packet's Level 3 contract does not require `decision-record.md`\n- [x] CHK-101 [P1] All ADRs have status — both are `Proposed`\n- [x] CHK-102 [P1] Alternatives documented with rejection rationale — ADR-001 rejects the hard error and the quiet-overrides-JSON variant; ADR-002 rejects the bare integer\n- [x] CHK-103 [P2] Migration path documented — N/A: the flag is additive and no caller has to change\n<!-- /ANCHOR:arch-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n## L3+: Performance Verification\n\n- [ ] CHK-110 [P1] Response time targets met (NFR-P01) — one extra string comparison; confirm the script still finishes under 100 ms\n- [x] CHK-111 [P1] Throughput targets met (NFR-P02) — N/A: no NFR-P02 is defined and the script is a one-shot CLI\n- [x] CHK-112 [P2] Load testing completed — N/A: no service surface\n- [x] CHK-113 [P2] Performance benchmarks documented — N/A: NFR-P01 is an order-of-magnitude bound, not a benchmark\n<!-- /ANCHOR:perf-verify -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n## L3+: Deployment Readiness\n\n- [ ] CHK-120 [P0] Rollback procedure documented and tested — documented in `plan.md` §7 and §L2; the revert is exercised only if a criterion fails\n- [x] CHK-121 [P0] Feature flag configured — N/A: `--quiet` is a caller-controlled opt-in, so no deployment flag is needed\n- [x] CHK-122 [P1] Monitoring/alerting configured — N/A: an internal CLI with no telemetry surface\n- [x] CHK-123 [P1] Runbook created — N/A: `show_help()` is the runbook for a one-flag script\n- [x] CHK-124 [P2] Deployment runbook reviewed — N/A: nothing is deployed\n<!-- /ANCHOR:deploy-ready -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:compliance-verify -->\n## L3+: Compliance Verification\n\n- [x] CHK-130 [P1] Security review completed — N